\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Overview of the G-theory evaluation pipeline. A factorial design crossing seven facets across eight models and five benchmarks produces 2.3M binary records. G-study variance decomposition via Henderson Method~I reveals that item difficulty (34--38\%) and model$\times $item interaction (30--36\%) together dominate in MC benchmarks. D-study projections translate variance components into design-dependent item budgets: a 3-prompt $\times $ 3-seed design achieves $G \geq 0.80$ with 178 items (3.1$\times $ reduction). Three contributions emerge: reproducibility budgets (C1), dominant item-level variance (C2), and format-associated variance patterns (C3).}}{2}{figure.caption.1}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:overview}{{1}{2}{Overview of the G-theory evaluation pipeline. A factorial design crossing seven facets across eight models and five benchmarks produces 2.3M binary records. G-study variance decomposition via Henderson Method~I reveals that item difficulty (34--38\%) and model$\times $item interaction (30--36\%) together dominate in MC benchmarks. D-study projections translate variance components into design-dependent item budgets: a 3-prompt $\times $ 3-seed design achieves $G \geq 0.80$ with 178 items (3.1$\times $ reduction). Three contributions emerge: reproducibility budgets (C1), dominant item-level variance (C2), and format-associated variance patterns (C3)}{figure.caption.1}{}}
